  "notes": "An older, somewhat informal word for licking or sucking on something. More commonly heard in regional dialects (especially western Japan) and in older usage. The standard modern equivalent is {舐|な}める.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {飴|あめ}をねぶる: to suck on candy\n- {指|ゆび}をねぶる: to lick one's finger\n- {唇|くちびる}をねぶる: to lick one's lips\n\nREGISTER:\nInformal and somewhat old-fashioned or dialectal. In standard modern Japanese, {舐|な}める is more common. However, ねぶる appears in literature and is still used in some dialects.\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {舐|な}める: to lick (standard, more common)\n- しゃぶる: to suck on (implies keeping something in the mouth)\n- {嘗|な}める: to taste, to lick (literary variant of {舐|な}める)",
